= Module 8: Spatial Operators
<module-8-spatial-operators>
== Learning Objectives
<learning-objectives>
After completing this module, you will be able to:

- Extract spatial subsets using coordinate boxes and grid indices
- Remap data between different grid types and resolutions
- Describe, set, and manipulate grids
- Compute spatial statistics (field means, maxima, etc.)
- Work with different grid types (regular, Gaussian, reduced,
  curvilinear, unstructured)
- Perform area-weighted statistics

== Prerequisites
<prerequisites>
This module assumes you have completed Modules 1-7 or are familiar with
basic CDO commands and have a working NetCDF file with spatial
dimensions (latitude/longitude).

`{note} You will need a global or regional dataset with latitude and longitude coordinates for the examples in this module. A global temperature or precipitation field works well. Sample files are available from the course data repository.`

#figure(image("../../assets/fig_ethiopia_precipitation.png"),
  caption: [
    Real CHIRPS precipitation climatology for Ethiopia, used by the
    spatial and climate-index examples in this course.
  ]
)

#figure(image("../../assets/fig_ethiopia_season_map.png"),
  caption: [
    Seasonal precipitation climatology over Ethiopia derived from the
    same real CHIRPS dataset: Belgi, Kiremt, Bega, and annual total.
    Regional subsetting and remapping of these fields is demonstrated in
    the examples below.
  ]
)

== 8.1 Spatial Domain Operations
<spatial-domain-operations>
Spatial domain operators subset data by geographical region or grid
index.

=== 8.1.1 Selecting by Latitude/Longitude Box
<selecting-by-latitudelongitude-box>
The `sellonlatbox` operator extracts a rectangular region:

```bash
cdo sellonlatbox,-10,40,30,70 europe.nc global.nc
```

The syntax is `sellonlatbox,x1,x2,y1,y2` where: - `x1` = western
longitude boundary - `x2` = eastern longitude boundary - `y1` = southern
latitude boundary - `y2` = northern latitude boundary

`{tip} For global data spanning 0-360°E, use longitudes in the 0-360 convention. For -180 to 180°E, use negative values for western longitudes.`

=== 8.1.2 Regions of Interest
<regions-of-interest>
```bash
# Tropical band
cdo sellonlatbox,-180,180,-30,30 tropics.nc global.nc

# Europe
cdo sellonlatbox,-10,40,30,70 europe.nc global.nc

# Africa
cdo sellonlatbox,-20,55,-35,38 africa.nc global.nc

# South America
cdo sellonlatbox,-85,-30,-60,15 south_america.nc global.nc

# Southeast Asia
cdo sellonlatbox,90,150,-15,30 se_asia.nc global.nc
```

#raw("{warning} `sellonlatbox` preserves the original grid structure but reduces the grid dimensions. It does **not** remap or interpolate — it simply crops.")

=== 8.1.3 Selecting by Grid Index Box
<selecting-by-grid-index-box>
When you know the grid indices rather than coordinates:

```bash
cdo selindexbox,10,50,20,60 indonesia_subset.nc global.nc
```

The syntax is `selindexbox,ilon1,ilon2,ilat1,ilat2` --- all 1-indexed.

#raw("{note} `selindexbox` is faster than `sellonlatbox` because it avoids coordinate lookups. It is useful in automated scripts where grid indices are known in advance.")

=== 8.1.4 Selecting Specific Grid
<selecting-specific-grid>
```bash
cdo selgrid,1 multi_grid_file.nc grid1.nc
cdo selgrid,2 multi_grid_file.nc grid2.nc
```

This is useful for data stored on multiple grids (e.g., output from
models with different parameterizations on different grids).

=== 8.1.5 Subsampling
<subsampling>
```bash
cdo samplegrid,2 global.nc every_other.nc   # keep every 2nd grid cell
cdo samplegrid,4 global.nc coarse.nc         # keep every 4th grid cell
```

`{exercise} :label: ex-spatial-1 Extract the Horn of Africa region (32-52°E, 5°S-15°N) from a global precipitation dataset.`

#raw("{solution} ex-spatial-1 ```bash cdo sellonlatbox,32,52,-5,15 horn_of_africa.nc global_precip.nc cdo info horn_of_africa.nc ```")

== 8.2 Remapping and Regridding
<remapping-and-regridding>
Remapping (regridding) transfers data from one grid to another. This is
essential when comparing or combining datasets on different grids.

=== 8.2.1 Bilinear Interpolation
<bilinear-interpolation>
```bash
cdo remapbil,target_grid.nc source.nc remapped.nc
cdo remapbil,r144x72 source.nc remapped.nc    # to regular 144x72 grid
```

`{tip} Bilinear interpolation is fast and works well for smooth fields like temperature. It is less suitable for precipitation where values can change abruptly.`

=== 8.2.2 Bicubic Interpolation
<bicubic-interpolation>
```bash
cdo remapbic,target_grid.nc source.nc bicubic.nc
```

Bicubic interpolation uses 16 surrounding grid points and produces
smoother results than bilinear, but can overshoot in regions with sharp
gradients.

=== 8.2.3 Conservative Interpolation
<conservative-interpolation>
First-order conservative:

```bash
cdo remapcon,target_grid.nc source.nc conservative.nc
```

Second-order conservative:

```bash
cdo remapcon2,target_grid.nc source.nc conservative2.nc
```

`{warning} Conservative remapping preserves area integrals (e.g., total precipitation, energy fluxes). It is the preferred method for precipitation and flux variables. It requires that the source and target grid cell areas can be computed.`

=== 8.2.4 Distance-Weighted Interpolation
<distance-weighted-interpolation>
```bash
cdo remapdis,target_grid.nc source.nc distance_weighted.nc
```

=== 8.2.5 Nearest Neighbor
<nearest-neighbor>
```bash
cdo remapnn,target_grid.nc source.nc nearest.nc
```

Nearest neighbor preserves original values and is ideal for categorical
data (e.g., land-sea masks, vegetation types).

=== 8.2.6 Remapping Methods Comparison Table
<remapping-methods-comparison-table>
#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: (17.95%, 17.95%, 46.15%, 17.95%),
    align: (auto,auto,auto,center,),
    table.header([Method], [Operator], [Best For], [Conservation],),
    table.hline(),
    [Bilinear], [`remapbil`], [Smooth fields (T, Z, pressure)], [No],
    [Bicubic], [`remapbic`], [Very smooth fields], [No],
    [Conservative], [`remapcon`], [Fluxes, precipitation], [Yes (1st
    order)],
    [Conservative2], [`remapcon2`], [Fluxes (higher accuracy)], [Yes
    (2nd order)],
    [Distance], [`remapdis`], [General purpose], [No],
    [Nearest], [`remapnn`], [Categorical, binary fields], [No],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

=== 8.2.7 Generating Interpolation Weights
<generating-interpolation-weights>
For repeated remapping operations, precompute weights to save time:

```bash
cdo genbil,target_grid.nc source.nc weights_bil.nc
cdo gencon,target_grid.nc source.nc weights_con.nc
cdo genbic,target_grid.nc source.nc weights_bic.nc
cdo gendis,target_grid.nc source.nc weights_dis.nc
cdo gennn,target_grid.nc source.nc weights_nn.nc
```

Apply precomputed weights:

```bash
cdo remap,target_grid.nc,weights_bil.nc source.nc remapped.nc
```

`{tip} When remapping many files to the same target grid (e.g., multiple CMIP6 models), generate weights once and reuse them for all files. This can reduce processing time by 50-80%.`

=== 8.2.8 Target Grid Specification
<target-grid-specification>
Target grids can be specified in several ways:

```bash
# From an existing file
cdo remapbil,reference_grid.nc input.nc output.nc

# Regular grid using resolution string
cdo remapbil,r360x180 input.nc output.nc       # 1° x 1°
cdo remapbil,r720x360 input.nc output.nc       # 0.5° x 0.5°
cdo remapbil,r1440x720 input.nc output.nc      # 0.25° x 0.25°

# Using grid description file
cdo remapbil,mytargetgrid input.nc output.nc
```

The `rNXxNY` format defines a regular lon-lat grid with NX longitude
points and NY latitude points.

`{exercise} :label: ex-spatial-2 Remap a global 0.25° dataset to a 1° x 1° regular grid using conservative remapping. Compare the file sizes and total precipitation sums.`

#raw("{solution} ex-spatial-2 ```bash cdo remapcon,r360x180 highres.nc lowres.nc cdo info highres.nc | find \"precip\" cdo info lowres.nc | find \"precip\" cdo fldsum -timsum highres.nc total_high.nc cdo fldsum -timsum lowres.nc total_low.nc ``` Conservative remapping should approximately preserve the total precipitation.")

== 8.3 Grid Operations
<grid-operations>
These operators describe, set, and manipulate grids.

=== 8.3.1 Describing Grids
<describing-grids>
```bash
cdo griddes temperature.nc
```

This outputs a complete grid description including the number of grid
points, grid type, and coordinate bounds.

```bash
cdo gridtype temperature.nc      # grid type (regular, curvilinear, etc.)
cdo nlon temperature.nc           # number of longitudes
cdo nlat temperature.nc           # number of latitudes
cdo nlevel temperature.nc         # number of vertical levels
cdo npoints temperature.nc        # total number of grid points
cdo gridarea temperature.nc       # compute grid cell areas
```

=== 8.3.2 Setting Grids
<setting-grids>
```bash
cdo setgrid,newgridfile.nc data.nc data_with_grid.nc
cdo setgridtype,curvilinear data.nc curvilinear.nc
```

#raw("{warning} Setting a grid with incorrect parameters can silently corrupt spatial coordinates. Always verify with `cdo griddes` after setting.")

=== 8.3.3 Grid Cell Areas
<grid-cell-areas>
```bash
cdo gridarea temperature.nc cell_areas.nc
cdo fldsum cell_areas.nc total_area.nc
cdo output -fldsum cell_areas.nc
```

Grid cell areas vary significantly with latitude. Cells near the equator
are much larger than cells near the poles on regular lat-lon grids.

#raw("{tip} Grid cell areas are essential for correct flux calculations. Always use `cdo gridarea` rather than assuming constant cell size.")

=== 8.3.4 Grid Editing
<grid-editing>
```bash
cdo setx,lonitude.nc temperature.nc   # set new longitude values
cdo sety,latitude.nc temperature.nc   # set new latitude values
cdo setgridtype,regular temperature.nc regular.nc
```

== 8.4 Spatial Statistics
<spatial-statistics>
Spatial statistics reduce the spatial dimensions (lat/lon) to produce a
single value per time step.

=== 8.4.1 Field Mean (Spatial Average)
<field-mean-spatial-average>
```bash
cdo fldmean temperature.nc global_mean.nc
```

#raw("{warning} `fldmean` computes a simple arithmetic mean over all grid cells. For proper area-weighted averaging, use `cdo fldmean` with area weights or use `areaavg`.")

Comparison of spatial mean types:

```bash
cdo fldmean temperature.nc simple_mean.nc              # arithmetic mean
cdo areaavg temperature.nc area_weighted_mean.nc       # area-weighted mean
cdo fldmean -gridarea temperature.nc weighted.nc       # manual weighting
```

=== 8.4.2 Other Field Statistics
<other-field-statistics>
```bash
cdo fldmax temperature.nc max_per_timestep.nc          # maximum at each time
cdo fldmin temperature.nc min_per_timestep.nc          # minimum at each time
cdo fldstd temperature.nc std_per_timestep.nc          # spatial standard deviation
cdo fldvar temperature.nc var_per_timestep.nc          # spatial variance
cdo fldsum temperature.nc sum_per_timestep.nc          # sum over all cells
cdo fldrange temperature.nc range_per_timestep.nc      # max-min
cdo fldmedian temperature.nc median_per_timestep.nc    # median
```

=== 8.4.3 Tropical Mean Example
<tropical-mean-example>
Combine spatial subsetting with field statistics:

```bash
cdo fldmean -sellonlatbox,-180,180,-30,30 temperature.nc tropical_mean.nc
cdo output tropical_mean.nc
```

=== 8.4.4 Zonal Statistics
<zonal-statistics>
```bash
cdo zonmean temperature.nc zonal_mean.nc               # mean along each latitude band
cdo zonmax temperature.nc zonal_max.nc
cdo zonmin temperature.nc zonal_min.nc
cdo zonstd temperature.nc zonal_std.nc
cdo zonsum temperature.nc zonal_sum.nc
```

Zonal means are useful for examining the latitudinal structure of
climate fields.

#raw("{note} `zonmean` averages across all longitudes at each latitude, producing a 1D (lat, time) field from a 2D (lon, lat, time) field.")

=== 8.4.5 Meridional Statistics
<meridional-statistics>
```bash
cdo mermean temperature.nc meridional_mean.nc
```

== 8.5 Grid Types and Conversion
<grid-types-and-conversion>
Climate data comes in many grid types. CDO can handle all major formats.

=== 8.5.1 Regular Lat-Lon Grid
<regular-lat-lon-grid>
The simplest grid --- equally spaced latitude/longitude grid cells.

```bash
cdo griddes regular_grid.nc
```

```
gridtype  = lonlat
xsize     = 144
ysize     = 72
xfirst    = 0
xinc      = 2.5
yfirst    = -88.75
yinc      = 2.5
```

=== 8.5.2 Gaussian Grid
<gaussian-grid>
Used by many atmospheric models (ECMWF, NCEP). Latitude spacing is
non-uniform with higher resolution near the equator.

```bash
cdo griddes gaussian_grid.nc
```

```
gridtype  = gaussian
xsize     = 256
ysize     = 128
yfirst    = -88.927...
yinc      = variable (Gaussian latitudes)
```

=== 8.5.3 Reduced Gaussian Grid
<reduced-gaussian-grid>
A Gaussian grid with fewer longitude points near the poles. This is more
computationally efficient.

```bash
cdo griddes reduced_gaussian.nc
```

`{warning} Reduced Gaussian grids require special handling. Not all operators support them. Consider remapping to a regular grid first.`

=== 8.5.4 Curvilinear Grid
<curvilinear-grid>
Grid cells are not aligned with lines of constant latitude/longitude.
Common in regional climate models.

```bash
cdo griddes curvilinear.nc
```

=== 8.5.5 Unstructured Grid
<unstructured-grid>
Grid cells have no regular structure. Common in ocean models (MPAS,
FESOM) and some newer atmospheric models.

```bash
cdo griddes unstructured.nc
```

=== 8.5.6 Converting Grid Types
<converting-grid-types>
Convert reduced Gaussian to regular grid:

```bash
cdo remapbil,r360x180 reduced_gaussian.nc regular.nc
```

Convert curvilinear to regular:

```bash
cdo remapbil,regular_target.nc curvilinear.nc regular.nc
```

#raw("{tip} For conversions involving unstructured grids, you may need to provide additional grid information. Use `cdo griddes` on your source file to understand its structure before attempting remapping.")

== 8.6 Area Operations
<area-operations>
Area-weighted statistics account for varying grid cell sizes.

=== 8.6.1 Area-Weighted Average
<area-weighted-average>
```bash
cdo areaavg temperature.nc area_avg.nc
```

This is the same as computing grid cell areas and using them as weights
in a weighted average.

=== 8.6.2 Other Area Operators
<other-area-operators>
```bash
cdo areamax temperature.nc area_max.nc
cdo areamin temperature.nc area_min.nc
cdo areastd temperature.nc area_std.nc
cdo areasum temperature.nc area_sum.nc
```

#raw("{note} The `area*` operators internally compute grid cell areas. They are generally preferred over `fld*` operators for climate applications where grid cell size varies.")

=== 8.6.3 Manual Area Weighting
<manual-area-weighting>
For full control:

```bash
cdo gridarea temperature.nc areas.nc
cdo fldmean -mul temperature.nc areas.nc weighted_mean.nc
```

== 8.7 Practical Workflow Examples
<practical-workflow-examples>
=== 8.7.1 Regional Climate Extraction
<regional-climate-extraction>
Extract and analyze a region from global data:

```bash
cdo sellonlatbox,32,52,-5,15 precip.nc horn_africa.nc
cdo yearmon -fldmean horn_africa.nc annual_precip_horn.nc
cdo output annual_precip_horn.nc
```

=== 8.7.2 Multi-Model Regridding
<multi-model-regridding>
Regrid multiple models to a common 1° x 1° grid:

```bash
# Generate weights once
cdo genbil,r360x180 model1.nc weights.nc

# Apply to all models
for model in model1.nc model2.nc model3.nc model4.nc; do
  cdo remap,r360x180,weights.nc "$model" "regridded_$model"
done
```

=== 8.7.3 Global Mean Time Series
<global-mean-time-series>
```bash
cdo output -fldmean -sellonlatbox,-180,180,-60,60 temperature.nc
```

=== 8.7.4 Regional Masks
<regional-masks>
Create a mask for a region and apply it:

```bash
# Create mask (1 for region, 0 elsewhere)
cdo setctomiss,0 -sellonlatbox,32,52,-5,15 global.nc mask.nc
cdo setmissval,1 -eqc,1 mask.nc
# Apply mask to data
cdo mul region_mask.nc original_data.nc masked_data.nc
```

`{exercise} :label: ex-spatial-3 Create a mask for the Nile Basin region and compute area-weighted average precipitation over the basin.`

#raw("{solution} ex-spatial-3 ```bash # Approximate Nile Basin bounding box (extension assumed from course data) cdo sellonlatbox,28,42,-5,25 precip.nc nile_basin.nc cdo areaavg nile_basin.nc nile_avg_precip.nc cdo output nile_avg_precip.nc ```")

== 8.8 Hands-On Exercises
<hands-on-exercises>
=== Exercise 8.1: Crop and Analyze a Region
<exercise-8.1-crop-and-analyze-a-region>
`{exercise} :label: ex-spatial-4 From a global temperature dataset: 1. Extract the Mediterranean region (10°W-40°E, 30°N-50°N) 2. Compute the area-weighted mean temperature 3. Compute the spatial standard deviation 4. Identify the warmest and coldest grid points in the region`

#raw("{solution} ex-spatial-4 ```bash cdo sellonlatbox,-10,40,30,50 global_temp.nc mediterranean.nc cdo areaavg mediterranean.nc mediterranean_mean.nc cdo areastd mediterranean.nc mediterranean_std.nc cdo fldmax mediterranean.nc warmest.nc cdo fldmin mediterranean.nc coldest.nc ```")

=== Exercise 8.2: Regridding Between Resolutions
<exercise-8.2-regridding-between-resolutions>
`{exercise} :label: ex-spatial-5 1. Remap a 0.25° dataset to 1° using bilinear interpolation 2. Remap the same dataset to 1° using conservative interpolation 3. Compare the two results — how do they differ near coastlines?`

#raw("{solution} ex-spatial-5 ```bash cdo remapbil,r360x180 highres.nc bilinear_1deg.nc cdo remapcon,r360x180 highres.nc conservative_1deg.nc cdo sub bilinear_1deg.nc conservative_1deg.nc difference.nc cdo fldmean -abs difference.nc mean_abs_diff.nc ``` Conservative remapping better preserves gradients near coastlines, while bilinear may smooth them. The absolute difference field highlights regions of largest discrepancy.")

=== Exercise 8.3: Regional Average Computation
<exercise-8.3-regional-average-computation>
`{exercise} :label: ex-spatial-6 Compute the area-weighted average precipitation for: 1. The Amazon basin (approximate: 80°W-50°W, 20°S-5°N) 2. The Congo basin (10°E-30°E, 10°S-5°N) 3. Southeast Asia (90°E-150°E, 15°S-15°N) Compare the seasonal cycles.`

\``{solution} ex-spatial-6`bash cdo areaavg -sellonlatbox,-80,-50,-20,5
global\_precip.nc amazon.nc cdo areaavg -sellonlatbox,10,30,-10,5
global\_precip.nc congo.nc cdo areaavg -sellonlatbox,90,150,-15,15
global\_precip.nc seasia.nc

= Compute monthly climatology for each
<compute-monthly-climatology-for-each>
cdo ymonmean amazon.nc amazon\_clim.nc cdo ymonmean congo.nc
congo\_clim.nc cdo ymonmean seasia.nc seasia\_clim.nc

= Output for comparison
<output-for-comparison>
cdo output amazon\_clim.nc cdo output congo\_clim.nc cdo output
seasia\_clim.nc

```
```

=== Exercise 8.4: Multi-Model Remapping
<exercise-8.4-multi-model-remapping>
`{exercise} :label: ex-spatial-7 Given three CMIP6 model files on different grids, remap all three to a common 2° x 2° grid and compute the ensemble mean.`

#raw("{solution} ex-spatial-7 ```bash TARGET=\"r180x90\" cdo remapbil,$TARGET model_A.nc model_A_r.nc cdo remapbil,$TARGET model_B.nc model_B_r.nc cdo remapbil,$TARGET model_C.nc model_C_r.nc cdo ensmean model_A_r.nc model_B_r.nc model_C_r.nc ensemble_mean.nc ```")

=== Exercise 8.5: Global Mean Temperature
<exercise-8.5-global-mean-temperature>
`{exercise} :label: ex-spatial-8 Compute the global mean surface temperature (GMST) from ERA5 data for 1981-2010. Compare the area-weighted mean with the simple arithmetic mean. How large is the difference?`

\``{solution} ex-spatial-8`bash \# Simple mean cdo fldmean -timmean
era5\_t2m.nc simple\_gmst.nc cdo output simple\_gmst.nc

= Area-weighted mean
<area-weighted-mean>
cdo areaavg -timmean era5\_t2m.nc weighted\_gmst.nc cdo output
weighted\_gmst.nc

= Difference
<difference>
cdo sub simple\_gmst.nc weighted\_gmst.nc diff.nc cdo output diff.nc

```
The difference is typically 1-3 K because polar regions (with small grid cells) have different temperatures than equatorial regions (with large grid cells). The area-weighted mean is more physically correct.
```

== 8.9 Quiz
<quiz>
\`\`\`{exercise} Question 1 :label: q-spatial-1

What does `cdo sellonlatbox,-10,40,30,70 global.nc` do?

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "a)", start: 1)
+ Selects longitudes 30-70 and latitudes -10 to 40
+ Selects longitudes -10 to 40 and latitudes 30 to 70
+ Cuts the file to have exactly 10 longitude and 40 latitude points
+ Remaps the data to a 10x40 degree grid
]

````

```{solution} q-spatial-1
The correct answer is **b**. The syntax is `sellonlatbox,x1,x2,y1,y2` representing western, eastern, southern, and northern boundaries. So -10°W to 40°E and 30°N to 70°N (Europe region).
````

\`\`\`{exercise} Question 2 :label: q-spatial-2

Which remapping method should be used for precipitation data when
conservation of total mass is required?

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "a)", start: 1)
+ `remapbil` --- bilinear interpolation
+ `remapbic` --- bicubic interpolation
+ `remapcon` --- conservative interpolation
+ `remapnn` --- nearest neighbor
]

````

```{solution} q-spatial-2
The correct answer is **c**. Conservative interpolation (`remapcon`) preserves area integrals, ensuring that the total precipitation amount is conserved when moving between grids. Bilinear and bicubic methods do not conserve mass.
````

\`\`\`{exercise} Question 3 :label: q-spatial-3

What is the primary difference between `cdo fldmean` and `cdo areaavg`?

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "a)", start: 1)
+ `fldmean` works on fields, `areaavg` works on areas
+ `areaavg` computes an area-weighted mean, `fldmean` computes a simple
  arithmetic mean
+ `fldmean` computes means faster
+ There is no difference --- they are aliases
]

````

```{solution} q-spatial-3
The correct answer is **b**. `cdo areaavg` weights each grid cell by its area (which varies with latitude on regular grids), while `cdo fldmean` treats all grid cells equally. For proper climate analysis, `areaavg` is preferred.
````

\`\`\`{exercise} Question 4 :label: q-spatial-4

What is a reduced Gaussian grid?

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "a)", start: 1)
+ A grid with the same number of points at all latitudes
+ A Gaussian grid with fewer longitude points near the poles
+ A regular grid at reduced (lower) resolution
+ A grid that has been compressed using lossy compression
]

````

```{solution} q-spatial-4
The correct answer is **b**. In a reduced Gaussian grid, the number of longitude points decreases toward the poles, where meridians converge. This reduces computational cost without significant loss of accuracy.
````

\`\`\`{exercise} Question 5 :label: q-spatial-5

Why would you generate remapping weights with `cdo genbil` before using
`cdo remapbil`?

#block[
#set enum(numbering: "a)", start: 1)
+ Weights are required --- `remapbil` will not work without them
+ To save time when remapping many files to the same target grid
+ To improve the accuracy of the interpolation
+ Weights are only needed for conservative remapping
]

````

```{solution} q-spatial-5
The correct answer is **b**. Precomputing weights with `genbil` (or `gencon`, etc.) saves the weight calculation step for each remapping operation. When remapping 50 model outputs to the same grid, generating weights once and applying them 50 times can reduce total processing time by more than half.
````

== Summary
<summary>
In this module, you learned:

- #strong[Spatial subsetting];: `sellonlatbox`, `selindexbox`, `selgrid`
  extract regions by coordinates or indices
- #strong[Remapping];: `remapbil`, `remapbic`, `remapcon`, `remapcon2`,
  `remapdis`, `remapnn` transfer data between grids with different
  characteristics
- #strong[Weight generation];: `genbil`, `gencon` precompute remapping
  weights for efficient batch processing
- #strong[Grid operations];: `griddes`, `gridarea`, `setgrid`,
  `setgridtype` manage grid metadata
- #strong[Spatial statistics];: `fldmean`, `fldmax`, `fldmin`, `fldstd`,
  `fldvar`, `fldsum` reduce spatial dimensions
- #strong[Area-weighted statistics];: `areaavg`, `areamax`, `areamin`,
  `areastd`, `areasum` account for varying grid cell sizes
- #strong[Grid types];: Regular lat-lon, Gaussian, reduced Gaussian,
  curvilinear, and unstructured grids

`{tip} Remapping is one of the most common yet computationally expensive CDO operations. Always precompute weights when processing multiple files, and choose the remapping method that is appropriate for your variable type (conservative for fluxes, bilinear for smooth fields, nearest neighbor for categories).`

Proceed to Module 9 to learn about statistical operators for climate
data analysis.
